---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Association Testing, Confounding & Linear Mixed Models (LMM)";
const description = "A first-principles mathematical and algorithmic exploration of GWAS association statistics (OLS, Logistic, Score tests), power calculations, population stratification, and the computational evolution of mixed models from GEMMA to BOLT-LMM, fastGWA, and REGENIE.";
/* Derived from this page's own content, not guessed: prose at 200 wpm, plus 15 s per
   display equation and 3 s per inline one. These constants had drifted to ~2.5x the
   truth (this page: 727 prose words, 9 display and 41 inline equations). Part 2 moves
   the series into a content collection where this is computed, not written down. */
const readingTime = "8 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 5</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Computational</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      How do we statistically test millions of genetic variants across hundreds of thousands of individuals while controlling for cryptic relatedness, population stratification, and severe case-control imbalance? This deep dive explores the mathematical machinery of Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS): <strong>single-marker regression tests (OLS, Logistic, Score tests)</strong>, <strong>statistical power formulations</strong>, <strong>EIGENSTRAT Principal Component Analysis</strong>, <strong>Leave-One-Chromosome-Out (LOCO)</strong>, and the computational evolution of <strong>Linear Mixed Models (LMMs)</strong> from exact spectral solvers (GEMMA) to ultra-scalable biobank algorithms (<strong>BOLT-LMM</strong>, <strong>fastGWA</strong>, and <strong>REGENIE</strong>).
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#single-marker">1. Single-Marker Association Statistics: OLS, Logistic & Score Tests</a></li>
        <li><a href="#power">2. Statistical Power & Sample Size Derivations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#stratification">3. Population Stratification, PCA & Genomic Inflation (λ_GC)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#lmm-formulation">4. The Linear Mixed Model (LMM) Framework & LOCO</a></li>
        <li><a href="#algorithms">5. Computational Breakthroughs: BOLT-LMM, fastGWA & REGENIE</a></li>
        <li><a href="#meta-analysis">6. Multi-Cohort Meta-Analysis & Heterogeneity (Q, I²)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="single-marker">
      <h2>1. Single-Marker Association Statistics: OLS, Logistic & Score Tests</h2>

      <h3>1. Quantitative Traits: Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)</h3>
      <p>
        For a continuous trait <Katex tex="\mathbf{y}" /> (<Katex tex="N \times 1" />) and genotype dosage vector <Katex tex="\mathbf{x}_j \in \{0, 1, 2\}^N" /> for SNP <Katex tex="j" />, the linear model with covariate matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{Z}" /> (age, sex, top ancestry PCs) is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">OLS Marginal Estimator & Wald Test</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{Z}\boldsymbol{\gamma} + \beta_j \mathbf{x}_j + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \qquad \hat{\beta}_j = (\mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{Z}} \mathbf{x}_j)^{-1} \mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{Z}} \mathbf{y}, \qquad z_j = \frac{\hat{\beta}_j}{\text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{M}_{\mathbf{Z}} = \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{Z}(\mathbf{Z}^T \mathbf{Z})^{-1} \mathbf{Z}^T" /> is the projection matrix onto the orthogonal complement of covariate space.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>2. Binary Traits: Logistic Regression & Odds Ratios</h3>
      <p>
        For binary case-control traits (<Katex tex="y_i \in \{0, 1\}" />), logistic regression models the log-odds of disease probability <Katex tex="\pi_i = P(y_i = 1 \mid \mathbf{x}_i)" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Logistic Regression Link Function & Odds Ratio</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\operatorname{logit}(\pi_i) = \ln\left(\frac{\pi_i}{1 - \pi_i}\right) = \mathbf{z}_i^T \boldsymbol{\gamma} + \beta_j x_{ij}, \qquad \text{Odds Ratio (OR)} = \exp(\beta_j)" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>3. The Score Test (Rao / Lagrange Multiplier)</h3>
      <p>
        Fitting full iterative logistic regression models for 10 million SNPs is computationally prohibitive. The <strong>Score Test</strong> evaluates the gradient of the log-likelihood evaluated strictly at the null hypothesis (<Katex tex="H_0: \beta_j = 0" />):
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Score Statistic Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="S_j = \mathbf{x}_j^T (\mathbf{y} - \hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}_0), \qquad T_{\text{Score}} = \frac{S_j^2}{\mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{V}_0 \mathbf{x}_j - \mathbf{x}_j^T \mathbf{V}_0 \mathbf{Z} (\mathbf{Z}^T \mathbf{V}_0 \mathbf{Z})^{-1} \mathbf{Z}^T \mathbf{V}_0 \mathbf{x}_j} \sim \chi^2_1" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}_0" /> and <Katex tex="\mathbf{V}_0 = \operatorname{diag}(\hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}_0(1 - \hat{\boldsymbol{\pi}}_0))" /> are computed <strong>only once under the null model</strong>, enabling lightning-fast genome-wide testing.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="power">
      <h2>2. Statistical Power & Sample Size Derivations</h2>
      <p>
        Under the alternative hypothesis (<Katex tex="H_1: \beta \ne 0" />), the association test statistic follows a <strong>non-central chi-square distribution</strong> with 1 degree of freedom and noncentrality parameter (NCP) <Katex tex="\lambda" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Noncentrality Parameter (NCP) & Statistical Power</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\lambda = \frac{2N p(1 - p)\beta^2}{\sigma^2}, \qquad \text{Power} = 1 - \Phi\left(z_{\alpha/2} - \sqrt{\lambda}\right) + \Phi\left(-z_{\alpha/2} - \sqrt{\lambda}\right)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          For genome-wide significance threshold <Katex tex="\alpha = 5 \times 10^{-8}" /> (<Katex tex="z_{\alpha/2} \approx 5.451" />), achieving 80% statistical power (<Katex tex="z_\beta \approx 0.842" />) requires:
          <br/>
          <Katex tex="\lambda \ge (5.451 + 0.842)^2 = 39.60 \implies N \ge \frac{19.80}{p(1 - p)\beta^2}" />.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="stratification">
      <h2>3. Population Stratification, PCA & Genomic Inflation (λ_GC)</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Population Stratification</strong> occurs when allele frequencies and baseline disease prevalences differ systematically across ancestral subgroups. If not controlled, thousands of non-causal SNPs will display spurious genome-wide significance.
      </p>

      <h3>EIGENSTRAT Principal Component Analysis (Price et al. 2006)</h3>
      <p>
        Let <Katex tex="\mathbf{W}" /> be the <Katex tex="N \times M" /> standardized genotype matrix. EIGENSTRAT computes the top eigenvectors of the sample covariance matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{\Psi} = \frac{1}{M}\mathbf{W}\mathbf{W}^T" />:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Ancestry Principal Components</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{\Psi} \mathbf{v}_k = \lambda_k \mathbf{v}_k \implies \mathbf{u}_k = \mathbf{v}_k \sqrt{\lambda_k}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          Including the top 10–20 Principal Components (<Katex tex="\mathbf{u}_1, \dots, \mathbf{u}_{20}" />) as fixed-effect covariates in the regression model corrects for broad continuous ancestral variation.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Genomic Inflation Factor (λ_GC) & Q-Q Diagnostics</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Genomic Control Inflation Factor</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\lambda_{\text{GC}} = \frac{\text{median}(\chi_1^2, \chi_2^2, \dots, \chi_M^2)}{0.454936}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          In large cohorts (<Katex tex="N > 100,000" />), genuine polygenicity naturally inflates <Katex tex="\lambda_{\text{GC}} > 1.2" />, meaning Q-Q plots must be evaluated in conjunction with LDSC intercepts.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="lmm-formulation">
      <h2>4. The Linear Mixed Model (LMM) Framework & LOCO</h2>
      <p>
        While PCA controls for broad continental ancestry, it fails to account for <strong>cryptic relatedness</strong> (unrecognized cousins/siblings) and localized pedigree structure. <strong>Linear Mixed Models (LMMs)</strong> model individual relatedness directly:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Genome-Wide Linear Mixed Model Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{Z}\boldsymbol{\gamma} + \beta_j \mathbf{x}_j + \mathbf{g} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbf{g} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{A}), \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I})" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> Proximal Contamination & LOCO (Leave-One-Chromosome-Out)
        </div>
        <p>
          If candidate SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is included in the construction of GRM <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}" />, its causal effect is fitted twice (once as fixed effect <Katex tex="\beta_j" /> and once as part of random polygenic effect <Katex tex="\mathbf{g}" />). This causes <strong>proximal contamination</strong>, which systematically reduces statistical power.
          <br/>
          <strong>Solution (LOCO):</strong> When testing SNPs on chromosome <Katex tex="c" />, the GRM <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}_{-c}" /> is computed using variants from all chromosomes <em>except</em> chromosome <Katex tex="c" /> (Yang et al. 2014 Nature Genetics).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="algorithms">
      <h2>5. Computational Breakthroughs: BOLT-LMM, fastGWA & REGENIE</h2>

      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Generative Architecture</th>
              <th>Algorithmic Breakthrough</th>
              <th>Scalability Limit</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>BOLT-LMM (Loh et al. 2015)</strong></td>
              <td>Spike-and-slab mixture of 2 Gaussians: <Katex tex="\beta_k \sim p_1 \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_1^2) + p_2 \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_2^2)" /></td>
              <td>Monte Carlo REML + Conjugate gradient solver; computes <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}\mathbf{v}" /> in <Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(MN)" /> without storing <Katex tex="\mathbf{A}" />.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="N \approx 500,000" /> (UK Biobank)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>fastGWA (Jiang et al. 2019)</strong></td>
              <td>Infinitesimal LMM with sparse GRM thresholding</td>
              <td>Sets small relationship coefficients (<Katex tex="A_{ik} < 0.05 \to 0" />); solves sparse Cholesky factorization in linear time.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="N \approx 1,500,000" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>REGENIE (Mbatchou et al. 2021)</strong></td>
              <td>Step 1 stacked ridge regression; Step 2 linear regression (quantitative) or logistic score test with Firth/SPA fallback (binary)</td>
              <td>Divides genome into blocks with cross-validation in Level 0/1; avoids large matrix operations entirely (<Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(N)" /> memory).</td>
              <td><Katex tex="N > 1,000,000" /> (UKB Exome / All of Us)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="meta-analysis">
      <h2>6. Multi-Cohort Meta-Analysis & Heterogeneity (Q, I²)</h2>
      <p>
        When combining summary statistics across <Katex tex="K" /> independent cohorts, <strong>Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Fixed-Effects Meta-Analysis</strong> yields the minimum-variance unbiased estimator:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Fixed-Effects Meta-Analysis Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{meta}} = \frac{\sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k \hat{\beta}_k}{\sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k}, \qquad \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_{\text{meta}}) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k}}, \qquad w_k = \frac{1}{\text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_k)^2}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Cochran's Q & Higgins' I² Heterogeneity Statistics</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Heterogeneity Statistics (Cochran 1954, Higgins & Thompson 2002)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="Q = \sum_{k=1}^{K} w_k \left(\hat{\beta}_k - \hat{\beta}_{\text{meta}}\right)^2 \sim \chi^2_{K - 1}, \qquad I^2 = \max\left(0, \frac{Q - (K - 1)}{Q}\right) \times 100\%" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="I^2 > 50\%" /> indicates substantial inter-cohort heterogeneity, requiring random-effects meta-analysis (e.g. DerSimonian-Laird or Han-Eskin).
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Summary -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Key Mathematical Takeaways
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Association Tests:</strong> OLS for quantitative traits; Score tests for ultra-fast binary case-control screening.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Confounding:</strong> EIGENSTRAT PCA captures continental ancestry; LMMs eliminate cryptic relatedness.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>LOCO:</strong> Prevents proximal contamination by excluding candidate chromosomes from the GRM.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Modern LMMs:</strong> REGENIE and fastGWA scale to millions of participants via block-ridge regression and sparse matrix factorizations.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="algo-promo-card" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <h3>📖 Next Deep Dive: Bayesian Fine-Mapping & Causal Prioritization (SuSiE)</h3>
        <p>
          Continue to Part 6 to discover how to pinpoint exact causal variants within high-LD association loci using Wakefield ABFs, Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE), 95% Credible Sets, and statistical colocalization.
        </p>
        <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-bayesian-fine-mapping/" class="algo-promo-btn">
          <span>Continue to Part 6: Bayesian Fine-Mapping</span>
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} />
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
